Z||000015||Seanh||11-03-2000||09:46 PM||||I must agree. Pioneers are great speakers. I also know that JL makes some killer 4x10's. I just thought I would share this little information I came across. During lunch today I stayed at the shop to use the computer and was looking throught one of our cataloges. I found this thing. It is a 4x10 to 6x9 adaper. It says it allows 6x9 speakers to be easily mounted in GM 4x10 holes. They were like 24.99 for the set. We dont have any, nor ever order any. I was wondering if anyone had ever heard of these or maybe tried them? I am going to order a couple of sets next time we order and give them a try on my project car to see how easy they really are. Maybe if they work I could order a few and sell them, who knows. Seems like you would still have to remove the dash, but we'll see.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif"> <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.||||reg||1
Z||000016||fiero56||11-03-2000||10:59 PM||||I bought my 6x9 from a stereo shop that I am pretty confident with. They looked into the possibility of using the adapters for the Fiero, but it turns out they are designed to be used on the rear deck in GM vehicles, and will not work properly on the Fiero dash, something about clearances.<P>The 6x9 speakers in my dash sound awsome compared to the 12 year old 4x10 that were in it. The sound is much cleaner, and there is quite a bit more bass put out. I can turn up the bass and make the rear view mirror bounce. Its nothing like having a 10" sub, but is better than nothing. The Boston Acoustincs I bought required removing the dash, but that is not a terrible job. Also, clearance on the driver side is none. The speaker is jammed tight between the dash and frame. The speakers don't rattle, and neother does anything in the dash/car from "improperly" mounted speakers, the setup works perfect for me.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||fropuff||11-03-2000||11:44 PM||||I'm thinking of getting some 6x9s tomorrow.  Sony Xplode I'm thinking.  I'll make it a weekend project.  I needed to replace the stock 4x10s anyway; the paper cone rattles like a *****.  They sound like crap.  I've heard Sony Xplode are pretty good.  They are also cheap; even from CRUTCHFIELD!!  That's a little suprising.  I will get them tomorrow from Best Buy tomorrow, unless there is something that someone has to say or disagree with.  Fiero56: If you could give me a little more help on this projeect, it would be greatly appreciated.  I would like a step-by-step process on how to do this.  I imagine you have to cut and splice some wires for them to work.  That information would be helpful too.  Thanks a lot for all the help.  Unless there is something I missed, I'll get them tomorrow.  Thanks again.  ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Seanh||11-04-2000||12:12 AM||||Taking out the dash is easier then you would think. On your stock speakers, there will be a clip connecting to the speaker. You will have to cut it off. The wire going to terminal A on the clip is your negative, the one going to terminal B is positve. Sony Xplod is fairly good. Im a Pioneer/JL/Image Dynamics/Kenwood/M B Quart person myself, but Sonys are fair speakers also. Procedure for removing the dash is outlined well in the Haynes Manual.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">  <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>It you smoke after sex, your doing it way to fast.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Philphine||11-04-2000||12:41 AM||||i'm using 1 8" for now. i would choose it over 2 5 1/4s. i'm happy with it till i get in my truck (2 12s). you could try a decent dual coil 8" and power it from both sides of a stereo. didn't notice if you using the stock radio or not, or how you plan to up grade.<P>say as a bare bones attempt, you could power the front and rear l/r speakers using the front outputs of your stereo  (wired in parallel. you'd lose fading till you did an upgrade)  and the rear output power the dual coil sub.<P>then you have options. later buy a small amp to power the sub and rewire the speakers as they were originaly. you could even keep going and get a nicer and/or bigger amp for the sub, let the original amp power the front speakers and let the rears run off the stereo or wire them in parallel again to the small amp.<P>keep your eyes open for a nice multichannel amp thats affordable. i have a jensen ( i know not the best name but nice if you use it within it's limits. mine says 580 watts but i know it's 230 rms and don't expect it to blow down buildings) amp that has 4 50 watt channels and 2 15 watt channels. if you can find one it would be perfect for a budget system. use 2 high channels for the fronts, the 2 15 watt channels for the rears, and the other high channels (if you have the impedance to bridge it, 150 watts) for the sub. got 2 in my truck that do a pretty good job. worth looking for. if i come across another i'll use it in my cheap thrill fiero. discontinued model but if you came across one you could probaly get it for for a pretty good deal. <P>also there is  mail order place that sells older (refurb i think) soundstream 5 channel amps for a pretty good price. well worth trying to come up with the cash for. can't think of their name but they advertise in the home stereo mags.  <P>got kind of long but i was trying to work through the basic options. good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 11-04-2000).]||||reg||1
